WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:09.010
I'm going to be talking
about exploratory programming

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:13.480
in Quarto with nbdev. Just a
little bit about myself,

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:16.840
I've been a data scientist
for about 15 years.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.760
About half of that I've
been building machine

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:22.330
learning infrastructure and
tools for data scientists

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:23.770
and software engineers.

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:27.760
I worked at places like
DataRobot, AirBnB, GitHub.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:31.450
Somewhere along the way, I met
somebody named Jeremy Howard.

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:34.330
You might know him
from his popular course

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:36.160
on machine learning.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:37.340
It's called fast.ai.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:42.130
He also has a library
by the same name,

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:45.850
and most recently I've
been working on a project

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:50.200
from fast.ai called
nbdev and it's something

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:53.530
that Jeremy used as
kind of a secret weapon

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:56.530
for his personal
productivity and something

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:58.490
that I find completely
fascinating.

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:01.630
And it's really
something very profound

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:04.480
that I want to share
with you today.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.410
So what does this talk about?

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:11.320
So in this talk, I'm going to be
introducing nbdev and

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:14.320
some associated
tools kind of composes

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.160
like a dialect of Python.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:22.600
And these tools allow
me and other developers

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:25.820
to be very productive.

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:28.120
And this dialect
of Python focuses

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:34.120
on Python's dynamic
properties and really focuses

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.650
on REPL-driven development.

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:39.550
So that you can build
software with kind

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:41.590
of this REPL-driven
development and software

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:43.150
engineering in mind.

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:45.310
So the reason I'm
saying that is really

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:48.670
want you to keep an open mind
to new workflows and new ideas

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:50.530
and new conventions of
ways of doing things

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:52.390
that you might not
necessarily be used

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:53.900
to when you're seeing Python.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:56.140
And all of this is
supercharged by Quarto,

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:59.110
which I'll talk about as well.

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:03.340
So just to set the foundation,
I think a lot of people

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:05.380
can agree, like in
scientific computing,

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:08.169
but also in software
engineering in general,

00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:11.950
exploratory programming is
very critical to our workflows.

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:15.460
So at least in the
Python community,

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:19.090
this is very kind of
a source of tension.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:22.670
So this is a screenshot
from Joel Cruz,

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:26.560
"I Don't Like Notebooks" talk,
if you may have seen it.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:32.290
And Joel Cruz also spends a
lot of time in the Python show

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:35.530
and he goes back and forth
between VS Code and iPython

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:42.850
and kind of it's this the
debate of what tools to use

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:44.500
and whether to use
notebooks or not

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.990
are very indicative
of this tension

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:50.300
that people feel
in their tooling.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:52.900
They want to use
exploratory programming,

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:54.610
but they encounter a
lot of friction when

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:55.902
they have to go back and forth.

00:02:55.902 --> 00:02:58.870
You have to live in almost two
worlds, the exploratory world

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:03.730
and then the world of your text,
like static environment, which

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:09.550
is a text editor, which
which is really painful.

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:12.640
RStudio, I feel like
knew this from the beginning

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:18.070
and you know, I started off
as an R programmer.

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:22.780
And, you know, I feel like
some of this knowledge

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:24.290
was baked into RStudio.

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:27.250
So I don't think in this
audience, anything I'm saying

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:29.800
is controversial.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:34.210
Another kind of
unrelated observation.

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:37.050
So other than sort of
REPL-driven development

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.700
and exploratory
programming, we also

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:43.120
have amazing tools to document
code, things like R Markdown

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:46.870
and Jupyter Notebooks
where we can weave together

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:48.970
code and prose.

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:51.700
However, the interesting
observation is, at least

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:55.900
for Python, this is not being
leveraged to his full potential

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:59.230
for documenting code,
at least in Python land.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:03.400
A lot of people are copy and
pasting code into Markdown.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:05.560
In this case, what I'm
showing here on the screen

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:07.930
is scikit-learn documentation
and we have

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:11.800
code been copied and
pasted into docs, strings

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:15.190
and outputs of code in doc
strings which is not ideal.

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:17.529
You're copying and
pasting code into a kind

00:04:17.529 --> 00:04:20.829
of a static environment,
and that's very painful.

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:25.790
So the question is, is there
something we can do about this?

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:28.720
Can we have the
best of both worlds?

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:33.730
And the answer to that is nbdev
is something like that.

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:36.220
So nbdev brings
everything together

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:42.250
into one interface exploratory
programming, documentation,

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:45.070
even things like testing,
continuous integration

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:47.620
and also Python packaging.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:52.010
So I was so excited when
I experienced nbdev.

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:56.650
I've been using it for almost
three years and you know,

00:04:56.650 --> 00:04:59.290
I felt like it was kind
of this iPhone moment

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:02.890
to me where all of a sudden
this technology was all bundled

00:05:02.890 --> 00:05:05.450
into one thing and it was very productive.

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:11.100
So just to give
you an idea of what nbdev is.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:15.160
Let me do just a little
bit of live coding, which

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:19.780
I try not to do at talks,
but we will see how it goes.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:22.038
So what I'm going
to do here is I

00:05:22.038 --> 00:05:23.330
don't know if you can see this.

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:25.880
Let me zoom in a little bit.

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:28.240
On the right hand
side is a notebook.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:31.030
And what I'm going to do
is I'm going to just author

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:33.340
a very simple
Python module that's

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:36.850
playing card, like a deck
of cards playing card.

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:39.880
And the idea is I'll show you
just a little bit about nbdev

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:41.565
just to whet your appetite.

00:05:41.565 --> 00:05:42.940
So at the top of
the notebook you

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:45.640
have is directive is very
much like a Quarto directive

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:46.690
as you can see.

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:50.230
And this default
export default_exp

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:53.950
tells us what Python module
this notebook is going

00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:59.800
to be compiled to and then
the corresponding source code

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:00.560
is on the left.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:03.640
You see this is card pi,
which corresponds to card,

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:06.220
and then we have some
prose in the notebook

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:10.700
and then we have some
snippet of code this export.

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:13.180
So this signifies that
this is source code.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:16.330
So I'm going to go
ahead and I'm going

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:25.810
to go ahead and kind of do
this fast version of pretending

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:26.990
like I'm authoring code.

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:33.640
So I'm going to, I'm going
to create this card class

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:35.570
and I'm going to
export this code.

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:38.740
I'll go through it
in a little bit.

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:41.085
Just for brevity purposes,
I kind of created this code

00:06:41.085 --> 00:06:43.210
beforehand, so you don't
have to sit here and watch

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:46.420
me code because that's not
the point of the presentation.

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:48.640
But basically, just
imagine you're writing

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:50.060
some code in a notebook.

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:51.620
Some of it is source code.

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:55.630
So, you know, I create
this, I have this code here,

00:06:55.630 --> 00:06:57.940
I have some card.

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:02.620
Actually, this here might
be something I want to

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:04.430
and you might want
to have some prose.

00:07:04.430 --> 00:07:12.370
So, for example, you can create
a card like this, very simple,

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:16.175
and then you might want to show
the representation of cards.

00:07:16.175 --> 00:07:18.050
This is a three of
diamonds is kind of small.

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:19.842
I don't know whether
you can see it or not.

00:07:19.842 --> 00:07:23.050
And then you can go into other
things such as let's just write

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:24.170
some more prose here.

00:07:24.170 --> 00:07:28.720
So let's do a let's
do a Markdown cell

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:34.120
and say you can compare cards.

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:36.000
Like so.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:43.210
OK and then I might
want to do something

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:51.020
like create another card
and maybe compare it.

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:54.770
So let's just say this
should be bigger than that.

00:07:54.770 --> 00:07:55.780
So it's true.

00:07:55.780 --> 00:07:58.280
And then I might want
this to be a test.

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:01.780
So I might want to put a
test here and say, hey, this

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:03.650
should be this should be true.

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:06.310
And then actually
it may be here.

00:08:06.310 --> 00:08:09.290
I might want another test, but
I might want to hide this test.

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:11.320
So I might want
to say, OK, I want

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:18.208
to make sure that this is
represented the right way.

00:08:18.208 --> 00:08:20.000
And I might want to go
ahead and hide that.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:21.708
I'm going to put the
high directive here.

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:27.880
And that's it's
kind of very simple.

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:31.780
Now, what I'm going to do is I'm
going to do export and export.

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:34.120
And what we have is,
you see the source code

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:39.091
has been generated in the
Python module from the notebook.

00:08:39.091 --> 00:08:41.049
So you can think of
notebook as syntactic sugar

00:08:41.049 --> 00:08:42.400
for source code.

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:46.540
But then also at the same
time what we have is,

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:51.535
have tests so I can run nbdev
test here in the console

00:08:51.535 --> 00:08:53.410
and then you'll see
it'll go through and test

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:54.160
all the notebooks.

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:56.680
Now nothing failed, but let's
say something does fail,

00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:58.570
so I'm going to make
that an equal sign.

00:08:58.570 --> 00:09:02.860
You'll see that you
see a failure message

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:07.240
and it'll show you, hey, in this
notebook there is a failure.

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:10.990
OK, so great.

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:13.270
And then the next
thing I want to show

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:16.400
you is the documentation.

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:19.000
So let me make this
screen a little bit bigger

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:20.270
so you can kind of see it.

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:25.100
And so the documentation
looks like this.

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:27.698
This is generated
by Quarto as well.

00:09:27.698 --> 00:09:29.740
You know, you have this
very simple documentation

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:32.740
that shows you the example,
shows you the test hides cells

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:34.482
that you might not want to see.

00:09:34.482 --> 00:09:35.690
And so that's the gist of it.

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:37.273
That's kind of
whetting your appetite,

00:09:37.273 --> 00:09:40.300
just scratching the surface
of what nbdev is for.

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:44.176
So, to summarize.

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:48.790
So let's go back to the slides.

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:50.830
So when you create
this notebook,

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:52.990
that's when the magic starts.

00:09:52.990 --> 00:09:54.380
That's just the beginning.

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:59.170
So you so at that point where
you kind of create this code,

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:02.800
the first thing you get is, is
Python modules which you saw.

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:05.000
You can do actually a 
two-way sync between them.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:08.960
So you can make edits in the
plain text version of that.

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:12.260
And it syncs backwards
for quick edits.

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:14.860
And you can navigate with
your plain text editor

00:10:14.860 --> 00:10:19.070
if you like things like C
tags and things like that.

00:10:19.070 --> 00:10:23.380
So you know, you can develop
Python modules from notebooks

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:23.990
very easily.

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:26.060
So you can import things.

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:28.030
It works very
similar semantically

00:10:28.030 --> 00:10:30.590
to how you would master modules in general.

00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:34.370
The documentation
gets rendered with Quarto.

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:39.280
The README can even be
generated with a notebook which

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:42.670
which is really cool.

00:10:42.670 --> 00:10:45.640
And then tests are
also done for you

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:48.580
so you can write unit
test in your notebook

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:53.950
and nbdev comes kind of
prepackaged with everything

00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:54.830
you need to do CI.

00:10:54.830 --> 00:10:58.720
So when you create an
nbdev project on Github,

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:00.970
it comes with everything you
need already pre-bundled,

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:04.750
the CI is already turned on,
your tests are already running

00:11:04.750 --> 00:11:09.190
and similarly with the
Quarto documentation,

00:11:09.190 --> 00:11:12.280
the machinery to host
this on GitHub pages

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:13.238
is already turned on.

00:11:13.238 --> 00:11:14.780
So all you do is
create your project.

00:11:14.780 --> 00:11:16.822
You get a live site with
your documentation site,

00:11:16.822 --> 00:11:20.620
you get CI if that's
what you choose.

00:11:20.620 --> 00:11:23.410
And then you also get
Python packaging, nbdev

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:25.030
takes care of all
the boilerplate,

00:11:25.030 --> 00:11:27.910
a lot of the boilerplate
involved with Python packaging

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:29.540
and that can be
really significant.

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:33.140
So things like PyPi and Conda,
it takes care of that as well.

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:35.530
So the thing that's
really powerful

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:37.930
is usually, you
know, when you're

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:41.840
done writing code, what
do you what do you do?

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:45.280
A lot of times you might
create documentation,

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:47.360
maybe in another
effort, you know,

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:50.300
you might figure out, OK, like
how am I going to package this?

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:51.910
And that's a different effort.

00:11:51.910 --> 00:11:54.520
And then similarly,
you might think about

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:57.760
like, I need to set up my Ci, I
need to do all of these things

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:01.570
and also like maybe
clean up your test.

00:12:01.570 --> 00:12:04.030
But all of this comes for free.

00:12:04.030 --> 00:12:06.522
And the thing is, it's
all in one context.

00:12:06.522 --> 00:12:08.230
You're not switching
context all the time

00:12:08.230 --> 00:12:09.710
for all these different things.

00:12:09.710 --> 00:12:11.800
You're writing
software this way.

00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:14.320
And I want to say
that we've written

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:17.090
all kinds of different
software with nbdev.

00:12:17.090 --> 00:12:19.720
It's not just scientific
computing libraries

00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:24.370
like fast.ai, but it's also
terminal user interfaces, API

00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:24.890
clients.

00:12:24.890 --> 00:12:27.430
There's an API client
for a Python client

00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:29.870
for the GitHub API called ghapi.

00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:32.590
It's also written in nbdev.

00:12:32.590 --> 00:12:33.620
We've written all kinds

00:12:33.620 --> 00:12:35.060
of other infrastructure.

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:40.220
So a wide range of software
has been built with this.

00:12:40.220 --> 00:12:46.210
So it's quite it
is quite robust.

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:47.620
And as I mentioned,
you know, you

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:50.770
can think of notebooks kind of
as syntactic sugar for source

00:12:50.770 --> 00:12:51.320
code.

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:54.910
So this is a comment from
Eric, who works at Lyft.

00:12:54.910 --> 00:12:58.570
He uses this at production,
in production at Lyft,

00:12:58.570 --> 00:13:03.400
and he says, I can write docs,
test and code all in one place.

00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:05.470
And he also says, I
get a lot of skeptical

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:07.930
looks when I say the
source code is notebooks,

00:13:07.930 --> 00:13:10.370
but that's just syntactic
sugar for raw source code.

00:13:10.370 --> 00:13:14.440
So I mean, he really, really
understands kind of nbdev

00:13:14.440 --> 00:13:18.080
in that way. And really,

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:21.400
so, another thing, if you want
to kind of experience nbdev

00:13:21.400 --> 00:13:24.430
and try it, which I
encourage everybody to do,

00:13:24.430 --> 00:13:28.270
one kind of useful mindset
is to kind of realize

00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:30.170
it's a dialect of Python.

00:13:30.170 --> 00:13:32.390
This is not just
programming in notebooks.

00:13:32.390 --> 00:13:36.220
There's also a lot of
extension of Python extensions

00:13:36.220 --> 00:13:38.140
to the Python programming
language, language

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:38.990
that complement.

00:13:38.990 --> 00:13:42.970
And there's a library called
fastcore, which is also,

00:13:42.970 --> 00:13:47.290
not surprisingly, built in
nbdev, which makes it easier

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:49.690
to program in a notebook.

00:13:49.690 --> 00:13:53.170
Things like it makes sure
the tab completion works

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:55.780
even better for
things and it makes

00:13:55.780 --> 00:14:00.340
sure it allows you
to split up code

00:14:00.340 --> 00:14:03.160
in between cells where you
might not usually be able to,

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:05.500
and all kinds of other
things, which I won't get

00:14:05.500 --> 00:14:07.810
into in this talk because
fastcore is a completely

00:14:07.810 --> 00:14:10.060
different talk by itself
and is very interesting even

00:14:10.060 --> 00:14:12.790
on its own.

00:14:12.790 --> 00:14:15.380
So you might be wondering,
what does Quarto enable?

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:17.330
So what is Quarto
to fit into this?

00:14:17.330 --> 00:14:18.830
So it's actually
really exciting.

00:14:18.830 --> 00:14:22.930
Before Quarto we had a different
kind of fragmented system

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:25.120
for you know, we had
a separate project

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:28.420
for blogs in a separate
project for books

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:32.710
and a separate project
for documentation.

00:14:32.710 --> 00:14:34.643
And then we were
trying we were also

00:14:34.643 --> 00:14:36.310
trying to figure out,
well, if you what,

00:14:36.310 --> 00:14:39.250
if you want to document your
existing code basis not written

00:14:39.250 --> 00:14:42.640
in nbdev, can you use nbdev
because nbdev has

00:14:42.640 --> 00:14:46.660
a lot of nice sugar for
documenting existing code

00:14:46.660 --> 00:14:47.990
bases as well.

00:14:47.990 --> 00:14:51.380
And so now we have a common
framework for all of that.

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:53.723
You don't have your common
set of tools no matter

00:14:53.723 --> 00:14:55.390
what kind of format
you want to produce,

00:14:55.390 --> 00:14:58.570
you can use you do that
because of course it's also

00:14:58.570 --> 00:14:59.990
very stable infrastructure.

00:14:59.990 --> 00:15:04.030
Like we're very impressed
by JJ and his support

00:15:04.030 --> 00:15:04.910
on this project.

00:15:04.910 --> 00:15:08.230
He's been amazing in
kind of adding features,

00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:10.010
squashing bugs, you name it.

00:15:10.010 --> 00:15:12.350
It's, it's been special.

00:15:12.350 --> 00:15:14.617
And we can also
offload a lot of work.

00:15:14.617 --> 00:15:16.450
There's a lot of
commonality between nbdev

00:15:16.450 --> 00:15:20.110
and Quarto, like a lot of
directives that Quarto has.

00:15:20.110 --> 00:15:22.480
We, you know, we don't
have to do that anymore

00:15:22.480 --> 00:15:24.730
because Quarto is doing it.

00:15:24.730 --> 00:15:27.350
The notebook filters
are great, you know?

00:15:27.350 --> 00:15:33.160
JJ added new notebook filters,
you know, in part for nbdev,

00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:36.650
and that makes it
very customizable.

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:39.430
You can do anything
essentially with notebooks

00:15:39.430 --> 00:15:42.670
in that situation,
and then also there's

00:15:42.670 --> 00:15:45.080
a flexibility to use any
static side generator.

00:15:45.080 --> 00:15:48.270
So people want to use their own
static side generator like pandoc

00:15:48.270 --> 00:15:49.520
source or something like that.

00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:51.970
And you can, you can
do that with Quarto

00:15:51.970 --> 00:15:53.830
because Quarto is kind
of like this pandoc

00:15:53.830 --> 00:15:58.390
super processor where you can
mutate anything and create

00:15:58.390 --> 00:16:02.170
anything, which is amazing.

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:05.600
So the last thing I want
to say is give nbdev a try.

